Post-Viral Olfactory Dysfunction
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Sudden anosmia or hyposmia immediately following URTI | Parosmia (distorted smell) during recovery phase | Structurally normal nasal endoscopy (no polyps)

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-019
Reason for Visit: Acute panic attack and severe distress related to sudden inability to taste or smell food, requiring urgent clinical evaluation to exclude an acute central neurological event.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate acute-onset, isolated neurological deficits (anosmia) causing severe patient anxiety. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out an acute ischemic stroke, intracranial mass, or acute toxin exposure, preventing severe neurological deterioration prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I was eating dinner and suddenly realized I can't taste a single thing, and I can't even smell the strong bleach I used in the bathroom earlier.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a sudden onset of profound anosmia and hyposmia. The patient details preceding mild upper respiratory tract infection symptoms two weeks prior. There is a profound alteration in flavor perception (ageusia) and mild parosmia. The patient notes an absolute absence of significant nasal congestion during this chronic phase. There is no purulent post-nasal drip. The sudden realization of the deficit tonight triggered immense psychological distress regarding the loss of smell and fear of a stroke. Past medical history notes a prior episode of profound postoperative fatigue with decreased physical stamina and exertional exhaustion years ago, which fully resolved and is unrelated to tonight's presentation.
Functional Status: Fully independent.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute anxiety; inability to detect environmental hazards like gas leaks or spoiled food.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 140/88 mmHg | HR: 92 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Patient appears hypervigilant, actively sniffing various household items during the interview, demonstrating severe acute health anxiety.
Physical Exam: Rhinoscopy reveals clear nasal passages without polyps, significant mucosal edema, or purulent discharge. Neurological examination confirms intact trigeminal nerve sensation in the nasal cavity (patient flinches appropriately to a noxious stimuli like a cotton swab). Crucially, there is a completely normal neurological examination of all other cranial nerves (II-XII, excluding I). Motor, sensory, and cerebellar exams are completely intact.
Environmental Observations: Patient has left a gas stove element unlit while checking the smell of gas; immediate correction and safety instruction provided.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. No intranasal medications currently used.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Post-Viral Olfactory Dysfunction - Status: Stable neurological deficit, complicated by severe acute panic.
Differentials:

Acute Ischemic Stroke - Ruled out completely due to the isolated nature of the cranial nerve I deficit, with absolute preservation of all other cranial nerves, motor, and sensory functions.

Intracranial Mass (Olfactory Groove Meningioma) - Ruled out clinically as the acute primary cause due to the sudden subjective realization immediately following a known viral prodrome, though requires routine imaging for absolute exclusion.

Chronic Rhinosinusitis - Ruled out due to the clear nasal passages on examination and complete absence of congestion or purulent discharge.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High (Inability to detect gas/smoke).

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required immediate after-hours assessment due to severe acute panic precipitated by the sudden realization of a profound neurological deficit. Telephone triage correctly prioritized the necessity to exclude a subtle or evolving cerebrovascular accident. A comprehensive cranial nerve examination firmly isolated the deficit to the olfactory nerve alone, directly ruling out acute ischemic stroke or complex central pathology. This definitive clinical evaluation immediately halted the patient's panic attack, secured the environment against sensory-related hazards, and safely averted an unnecessary and highly stressful emergency department admission for a benign post-viral syndrome.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Reassurance provided regarding the non-life-threatening nature of the symptom. The expected investigation results include objective olfactory testing demonstrating a profound deficit with a normal non-contrast brain MRI, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis via the regular GP or ENT specialist.
Positive Behavioral Change: To initiate olfactory nerve recovery, begin "smell training" by actively sniffing four distinct, strong familiar scents (e.g., coffee, lemon, eucalyptus, clove) for 20 seconds each, twice a day; actively engaging this specific neural pathway stimulates the regeneration of olfactory receptor neurons.
Home-Based Services: None indicated.
DME/Safety: Explicit instruction to install additional interconnected smoke alarms and routinely check expiration dates on all food due to the loss of sensory safety mechanisms.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 2 weeks for formal ENT and potentially neurology referral.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops new visual changes, sudden weakness on one side of the face or body, slurred speech, or severe unremitting headache.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive; isolated cranial nerve deficits require formal outpatient neuroimaging for absolute definitive diagnosis.
Clinical Course: The expected natural course involves very slow, unpredictable recovery over months to years, with many patients regaining partial or distorted function before returning to baseline.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate emergency attention if you develop any sudden weakness, difficulty speaking, or if you begin to see double or lose your vision.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: We are not sending you for a brain scan tonight because an isolated loss of smell following a cold is a classic viral injury, not a sign of an active stroke or acute brain emergency.
Actionable Steps: Rely strictly on visual expiration dates for food safety and ensure your home gas appliances are fully maintained or switched to electric where possible.

